Subject: [PATCH] net: avoid pingpong delayed-ACK stalls on in-VM container and bridge traffic

Keep-alive request/response traffic inside the guest can stall for a
delayed-ACK timeout (~40-50 ms at HZ=100) when a server writes response
headers and body separately without TCP_NODELAY: katamaran gives eth0 a
65520-byte MTU for gvproxy throughput and local bridges/veths inherit it,
putting response bodies up to ~64 KiB below one MSS so Nagle waits for an
ACK while the peer in pingpong mode delays it.

Setting RTAX_QUICKACK=1 by rewriting live routes from userspace has
inherent TOCTOU races with Docker/CNI gateway updates and root route
switches, and drops hidden IPv6 kernel flags and expiry state on replace.
Instead, record dst eligibility in a spare bit of icsk_ack
(kat_quick_ack) inside sk_setup_caps() when a TCP socket's resolved dst
uses a non-init network namespace (Docker containers, Kubernetes pods,
LXC machines) or a local bridge/veth device in init_net (cni0, docker0,
br-*, katbr0), leaving all routing tables untouched.

To avoid forcing extra ACKs on Internet-bound or host-bound container and
pod connections whose packets leave/enter the guest through virtio-net
(eth0), tag skbs received in virtnet_receive_done() with
skb->kat_from_uplink (preserved across __copy_skb_header,
skb_scrub_packet, IPv4/IPv6 fragmentation metadata copies in
ip_copy_metadata/ip6_copy_metadata, and fragment reassembly in
inet_frag_reasm_finish, and cleared in napi_reuse_skb) and record it on
the socket in tcp_create_openreq_child(), tcp_finish_connect(), and
tcp_event_data_recv(). In-VM traffic (pod-to-pod, ClusterIP Service DNAT,
container-to-container, root-to-bridge) never crosses virtio-net, so
kat_from_uplink stays 0.

Rather than bypassing Linux's quickack budget in tcp_in_quickack_mode()
for the lifetime of every flow (which would double pure ACK traffic on
sustained bulk transfers), lift only the pingpong suppression in
__tcp_cleanup_rbuf() when userspace drains a small or PSH-terminated
segment (ICSK_ACK_PUSHED && !sk_rmem_alloc) on an eligible non-uplink
socket with READ_ONCE(kat_quickack_enabled), and handle the matching
SO_RCVLOWAT case in __tcp_ack_snd_check() when a pushed segment leaves
in-order data queued below sk_rcvlowat and tcp_epollin_ready() is false.

Pushed segments are classified in tcp_measure_rcv_mss() into two tiers
recorded in tcp_sock.kat_short_push (independent of the adaptive rcv_mss
estimate and cleared whenever an ACK covering everything received goes
out in tcp_event_ack_sent()):
- The upper bound `ours` is derived from our advertised MSS (tp->advmss,
  which already excludes timestamps after the handshake on active and
  passive opens, deducting TCPOLEN_TSTAMP_ALIGNED only during SYN_SENT /
  SYN_RECV), capped by half the largest receive window we advertised
  (tcp_sock.kat_max_adv_win, mirroring tcp_bound_to_half_wnd()).
- The peer hint `peers` is min(ours, tp->rx_opt.mss_clamp - opts).
  Because a peer's SYN MSS limits what it receives rather than what it
  sends (e.g. a low `advmss` route metric over a jumbo-MTU path),
  tp->rx_opt.mss_clamp is not used as a hard send ceiling.
- For PSH segments (judging the last, PSH-carrying slice of a GSO/GRO
  skb): seg_len < peers yields KAT_PUSH_SHORT (definite sub-MSS push);
  peers <= seg_len < ours yields KAT_PUSH_UNCERTAIN (asymmetric low peer
  advmss or a narrower-MTU peer); otherwise KAT_PUSH_NONE.

Our own send-side state (mss_cache) is not used. Classification and
window tracking run only on eligible sockets (classification also only
for non-uplink segments with kat.quickack enabled); all other sockets
pay a bit test. Below SO_RCVLOWAT:
- KAT_PUSH_SHORT gets an immediate ACK if no earlier cumulative ACK
  already covers unread data (!after(tp->rcv_wup, tp->copied_seq)).
- Otherwise, for KAT_PUSH_SHORT (such as the middle write of a 3-write
  Nagle exchange) or KAT_PUSH_UNCERTAIN (such as an asymmetric-advmss
  first write), stock ACK checks run and at most one pending delayed-ACK
  timer per unread batch (tracked in tcp_sock.kat_lowat_caps_used, reset
  in __tcp_cleanup_rbuf() whenever copied > 0, and cleared on disconnect
  and child-socket creation) is shortened to a short non-extending
  deadline (accounting for the timer wheel's +1 tick rounding so HZ=100
  fires on the next 10 ms tick rather than 40-50 ms).

Subsequent paced pushes in the same unread batch, full-sized PSH
segments below SO_RCVLOWAT, and sustained bulk streams keep stock Linux
delayed-ACK batching, once-per-two-segments ACK pacing, and quickack
budgets untouched. Toggling /sys/module/kat/parameters/quickack at
runtime applies to subsequent ACK decisions on new and established TCP
connections (timers already armed are not rescheduled) without affecting
routes that explicitly set RTAX_QUICKACK=1. Explicit locked route
metrics (RTAX_LOCK on RTAX_QUICKACK) are respected. Disable at boot with
kat.quickack=0.

